  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/10af2af004ec61307d14a56f61a4a14acc87ded9ed38c3168c6b165b412eb5f0.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=-5{x}^{2}(x-1{)}^{2}(x-2)?$",
  "worked_solution": "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=2$ is $1,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at this point. Moreover, because $x=2$ is a simple root, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the roots $x=0,1$ is $2,$ which is even, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at these points.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\text{I}$ and $\\text{IV}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is negative, as $x$ approaches $\\infty ,$ $y$ approaches $-\\infty .$\n\nOnly graph $\\text{IV}$ displays this end behavior. Therefore, the correct answer is $\\text{IV}.$",
